The SDK example already shows exactly what you need — a Node.js worker that runs 10 jobs concurrently and gives up on a non‑existent user by throwing `PermanentError` [1], [2], [3].

```ts
import { Harbor, PermanentError } from '@harborline/sdk'

const harbor = new Harbor({ apiKey: process.env.HARBOR_API_KEY, region: 'eu-central-1' })

const worker = harbor.worker(
  'emails',
  async (job) => {
    const user = await db.users.find(job.payload.userId)
    if (!user) throw new PermanentError(`user ${job.payload.userId} does not exist`)
    await mailer.send(user.email, job.payload.template)
  },
  { concurrency: 10 },
)
```

`concurrency: 10` makes the worker handle 10 jobs in parallel [2], [3]. An ordinary error would retry, but `PermanentError` skips remaining attempts and moves the job straight to `dead` — no retry, exactly “giving up right away” [1].

Keep your API key out of source code by reading it from an environment variable like `process.env.HARBOR_API_KEY` at startup.
